with q as (
  select $l29_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why can a distant astronomer, seeing Earth only as a dim point of light, still tell that it is alive?", "options": ["Because Earth is bluer than lifeless planets", "Because life has so thoroughly remade Earth's atmospheric chemistry that its imprint can be read in the planet's spectrum \u2014 most tellingly, oxygen and methane coexisting though they destroy each other, a disequilibrium no lifeless world could sustain", "Because Earth reflects more light than a dead planet", "Because they can pick up our radio broadcasts"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A biosignature is a measurable fingerprint that distinguishes an inhabited world from a lifeless one. Life is not a passive passenger \u2014 it reshapes its planet's chemistry on a planetary scale (Earth's 21% oxygen is entirely life's product). So even seen as an unresolved dot, Earth betrays itself in its spectrum: oxygen and methane persist together though they react and destroy each other, a chemical impossibility that only a living planet, ceaselessly replenishing both, could maintain."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is 'chemical disequilibrium,' and why is it called the gold-standard biosignature \u2014 even for alien biochemistries?", "options": ["An atmosphere with lots of one gas; it only works for Earth-like life", "The persistence of reactive gases that should have reacted away and vanished (like O\u2082 and CH\u2084), held out of equilibrium only because life continuously replenishes them \u2014 and because it detects the thermodynamic signature of a DRIVEN system rather than any specific molecule, it could betray life of a chemistry we cannot even guess", "The point where an atmosphere stops reacting; it means the planet is dead", "A balance of gases that proves photosynthesis specifically"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A lifeless atmosphere runs downhill to equilibrium \u2014 reactive gases react until only a placid, unreactive mix remains (CO\u2082-dominated Venus and Mars). Life is a chemical engine that pumps reactive gases in faster than chemistry removes them, holding the air away from equilibrium \u2014 a ball kept partway up a hill. A persistent disequilibrium means something is doing continuous work to maintain it, most likely life. Crucially it detects the thermodynamic fact of a driven system, not any one molecule, so it is a biosignature for life-as-we-don't-know-it, not just life like ours."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How does transit spectroscopy read the atmosphere of a planet light-years away?", "options": ["A probe flies to the planet and samples the air directly", "When the planet crosses in front of its star, a thin sliver of starlight passes THROUGH the planet's atmosphere on the way past its edge; the gases absorb their characteristic wavelengths, stamping their spectral barcode onto that light, which we extract by comparing the star's spectrum during transit to its spectrum outside transit", "The planet's own glow is spread into a spectrum and read directly", "Radio telescopes pick up the atmosphere's emissions"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Spectroscopy reads composition from light: every gas absorbs at characteristic wavelengths, a unique barcode. During a transit, most starlight comes straight to us but a sliver grazes through the ring of the planet's atmosphere, and the gases imprint their absorption lines on it. Subtracting the out-of-transit (pure star) spectrum from the in-transit spectrum isolates the atmosphere's fingerprint. The signal is minuscule \u2014 a few parts per million for an Earth-like world \u2014 which is why it sits at the edge of what even JWST and its successors can do."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the 'false-positive problem,' and why does it mean a single biosignature is almost never conclusive?", "options": ["Instruments sometimes malfunction; you just repeat the measurement", "Essentially every biosignature gas has a lifeless, purely chemical way to be produced \u2014 oxygen from starlight splitting water or CO\u2082, methane from volcanism or water-rock serpentinisation \u2014 so any single gas could be a forgery, and a claim of 'biosignature, therefore life' must survive the question: could this have been made without life? (usually: possibly yes)", "Life sometimes fails to produce any biosignature at all", "Distant planets are too faint to measure reliably"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A false positive is a lifeless process that mimics a biosignature, producing the same fingerprint life would. Oxygen, the 'best' biosignature, can build up abiotically when UV splits water and the hydrogen escapes to space (as may have happened on Venus), or by splitting CO\u2082. Methane can come from volcanism or serpentinisation (the same water-rock chemistry that makes Enceladus's hydrogen). So a single gas is intriguing but not proof \u2014 nature forges the fingerprints of life, and from starlight alone the forgery is hard to distinguish from the real thing."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is a combination of biosignatures in disequilibrium (like O\u2082 AND CH\u2084 together) so much stronger than either gas alone?", "options": ["Two measurements are simply more precise than one", "Because a lifeless explanation would now have to produce BOTH gases simultaneously and hold them in mutual chemical conflict \u2014 and independent flukes multiply, so the combined probability of a coincidental abiotic origin collapses far below that of either gas by itself", "Because oxygen cancels out methane's false positives chemically", "Because only one of the two needs to be biological"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Nature can forge oxygen, and nature can forge methane \u2014 but to forge both at once, in the same air, held in a disequilibrium where each is destroying the other, it must run two independent tricks in concert. Independent probabilities multiply: if oxygen has (say) a 20% chance of being abiotic and methane a 30% chance, both being an abiotic coincidence is roughly 0.2 \u00d7 0.3 = 6%, far below either alone. This is the whole strategy against false positives \u2014 never trust one molecule; hunt for combinations in disequilibrium, plus habitable context, that a lifeless world would have to fake several times over."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why detecting life is 'building a case, not flipping a switch.' State the false-positive problem and why single biosignatures fail, then explain the three tools that build confidence \u2014 combinations, context, and the standard of evidence (Sagan's dictum and the confidence scale) \u2014 and why even a strong remote detection tops out at 'confident' rather than a triumphant 'life confirmed.'", "rubric": "THE FALSE-POSITIVE PROBLEM: a false positive is a lifeless, purely chemical/physical process that MIMICS a biosignature, producing the same fingerprint life would, so a signal we hoped meant 'life' may mean only 'unusual geology/chemistry.' Essentially EVERY proposed biosignature gas has plausible ABIOTIC sources: OXYGEN (the 'best' biosignature) can build up abiotically via PHOTOLYSIS \u2014 UV splits H2O and the light hydrogen escapes to space leaving O2 (as may have happened on Venus losing its oceans), or UV splits CO2; METHANE can come from VOLCANISM or SERPENTINISATION (water reacting with rock \u2014 the same water-rock chemistry that makes Enceladus's hydrogen), with no life. So a SINGLE biosignature is almost never conclusive \u2014 intriguing/suggestive but not proof \u2014 and any 'biosignature therefore life' claim must survive the question 'could this have been made without life?', whose honest answer is usually 'possibly yes.' THE THREE TOOLS: (1) COMBINATIONS \u2014 look for signatures hard to forge TOGETHER. O2 alone has a forgery and CH4 alone has a forgery, but BOTH coexisting IN DISEQUILIBRIUM demands an abiotic story that makes both gases simultaneously and holds them in mutual chemical conflict; because independent flukes MULTIPLY, the combined probability of a coincidental abiotic origin collapses far below either gas alone. The more independent hard-to-forge signatures coincide, the harder the lifeless explanation. (2) CONTEXT \u2014 a biosignature is far more convincing on a world INDEPENDENTLY known to be habitable (right size, in the habitable zone, water vapour present, orbiting a quiet star that doesn't manufacture abiotic oxygen). The same gas is more or less convincing depending on everything else known; detection is holistic detective work, not one dial. (3) STANDARD OF EVIDENCE \u2014 Sagan's dictum: EXTRAORDINARY CLAIMS REQUIRE EXTRAORDINARY EVIDENCE. Finding life would be among the most consequential claims in science, so the bar is very high: robust detection (not an artifact/fluke), abiotic sources ruled out, combinations + context, ideally INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION. The field formalises this as a CONFIDENCE SCALE / ladder of evidence (e.g. from 'intriguing candidate signal' up through 'abiotic sources disfavoured' to 'confident detection') \u2014 explicitly treating detection as a GRADED ACCUMULATION of confidence, not a single yes/no moment. WHY EVEN A STRONG DETECTION IS ONLY 'CONFIDENT': because no remote measurement can absolutely exclude every lifeless alternative, and the claim is so extraordinary; so honest cases top out at 'confident,' and a single real gas often stalls at 'candidate' (a middle rung), which is exactly where honest teams have had to announce genuine detections. A strong answer frames the whole thing as: detecting life is not finding a magic molecule but assembling multiple mutually-reinforcing lines of evidence, ruling out forgeries, until the lifeless alternatives become implausible \u2014 a case built over time, so the FIRST detection will be contested and gradual, not a sudden eureka, and that rigor is the search's INTEGRITY, what makes the eventual answer trustworthy."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Astrobiology has been 'burned and learned.' Recount the cautionary cases \u2014 Viking (1976), the ALH84001 meteorite (1996), Venus phosphine (2020), K2-18b's DMS, and the still-unresolved methane on Mars \u2014 and explain what each teaches about the difficulty of biosignature detection and why the discipline demands such patience.", "rubric": "THE PATTERN (thesis): astrobiology's real content is EPISTEMOLOGY under an overwhelming desire to believe; the field has been embarrassed publicly several times and each time TIGHTENED its standards \u2014 the history is the evidence for why the confidence scale and 'extraordinary evidence' standard exist. THE CASES: (1) VIKING (1976) \u2014 NASA's landers ran the first in-situ life-detection experiments on Mars; the Labeled Release experiment fed nutrients to the soil and got a POSITIVE-looking signal (something metabolised). But it was inconsistent with the other instruments (which found NO organic molecules), and the consensus became that the signal was ABIOTIC \u2014 from unexpectedly reactive soil chemistry, later understood to involve PERCHLORATES (oxidising salts that mimic metabolism with no cell present). Lesson: even a purpose-built life-detection test, on samples directly in hand, can be FOOLED \u2014 in-situ detection is not immune to false positives. (2) ALH84001 (1996) \u2014 a claim of fossilised microbes (tiny tube-like structures) inside a Martian meteorite, so momentous the US President announced it from the WHITE HOUSE LAWN; it then unravelled as the 'fossils' had plausible NON-BIOLOGICAL explanations. Lesson: a MORPHOLOGICAL false positive \u2014 SHAPE is the easiest fingerprint to fake; life-like structure is not life. (3) VENUS PHOSPHINE (2020) \u2014 a reported detection of phosphine (a candidate biosignature with no good abiotic Venus source) made worldwide headlines; other teams RE-REDUCED the same data and the signal FADED \u2014 marginal at best, tangled with subtracting instrument artefacts. Lesson: a claimed signal must be REAL (survive independent reanalysis), not an artifact; 'did not survive contact with other people's software.' (4) K2-18b / DMS \u2014 an exoplanet atmosphere reported to show dimethyl sulphide (on Earth made almost only by marine life); the feature was FAINT and did not reliably survive independent pipelines \u2014 it remains CONTESTED, a candidate at most. Lesson: faint exoplanet features are easily over-read; independent confirmation is essential, and honest results get held to 'candidate' (often ~level 3 of 7), not 'life.' (5) MARS METHANE (live/unresolved) \u2014 orbiters and the Curiosity rover have detected fluctuating methane implying an active present-day source (methane should be destroyed over time). It could be BIOLOGICAL (methanogens, as at Enceladus) or GEOLOGICAL (serpentinisation \u2014 water+rock, no life), and from the data WE CANNOT TELL WHICH, even with spacecraft AT Mars after ~two decades (even the detections themselves have been debated as instruments disagree). Lesson: even NEARBY, with landers on the ground, a potential biosignature can be maddeningly ambiguous. WHY PATIENCE/RIGOR: if we cannot resolve methane's source on our OWN neighbour with probes on the surface, interpreting a biosignature on an exoplanet light-years away from starlight alone is VASTLY harder \u2014 so exoplanet claims must be held to an extremely high, cautious standard; we should expect ambiguity, demand combinations/context, rule out abiotic sources, and accept that the first real detection will be a long CONTESTED process, not a sudden certainty. A strong answer ties the cases together as one lesson \u2014 a single biosignature is never enough, signals must be real and abiotic sources excluded, and the demand for extraordinary evidence is the field's INTEGRITY (the discipline that separates discovery from wishful thinking), NOT a weakness."}]$l29_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/space-exploration/astrobiology/biosignatures', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
